    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the difference between high and low glycemic index foods and how they impact insulin response? I need to put this in a briefing for a client who just got diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High and low glycemic index (GI) foods refer to how quickly they cause a spike in blood sugar levels after consumption. High-GI foods, such as white bread and sugary snacks, cause a rapid increase in insulin production, leading to an insulin surge. This can be challenging for individuals with Type 2 diabetes to manage, as it can trigger insulin resistance and worsen the condition. Low-GI foods, on the other hand, cause a gradual increase in insulin production, promoting a more balanced glucose metabolism and better insulin sensitivity.",
