      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you give me some specific examples of high-GI versus low-GI foods? I want to make sure the list I give my client is easy to follow and covers common staples like grains and fruits.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a list of examples of high-GI and low-GI foods that your client can reference:\n\n**High-GI Foods:**\n- White bread, white rice, pasta\n- Sugary snacks: cookies, cakes, candy\n- Fresh fruit: bananas, grapes, mandarin oranges\n- Dried fruit: dates, prunes, raisins\n\n**Low-GI Foods:**\n- Whole grains: brown rice, quinoa, whole-wheat bread\n- Non-starchy vegetables: leafy greens, broccoli, bell peppers\n- Fresh fruit: apples, berries, citrus fruits\n- Legumes: lentils, chickpeas, black beans\n\nKeep in mind, the GI index can vary depending on ripeness, meal composition, and serving size, so it's recommended to provide this list as a starting point for your client's discussion.",
